      heading: "What blood tests and checks do you need on testosterone?",
      body: `<p><strong>The core test is total testosterone - before starting and a few weeks after - to keep levels within the female range. Clinicians may also check SHBG, and consider lipids and other tests based on your health. Equally important are regular checks for acne, hair growth, voice change and mood.</strong></p>
<table><tr><th>Check</th><th>Why</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>Total testosterone</td><td>Confirm levels stay in the female range</td><td>Baseline, about 3-6 weeks, then periodically</td></tr><tr><td>SHBG (sometimes)</td><td>Helps interpret levels, especially on oral estrogen</td><td>Baseline</td></tr><tr><td>Lipids, blood pressure</td><td>Cardiovascular baseline</td><td>Baseline and as advised</td></tr><tr><td>Side-effect review</td><td>Catch androgenic effects early</td><td>Each follow-up</td></tr><tr><td>Breast screening</td><td>Routine mammography continues</td><td>As scheduled</td></tr><tr><td>Medication review</td><td>Testosterone can affect blood thinners and blood sugar control</td><td>When starting or changing medicines</td></tr></table>
<p>One technical note: lab tests designed for men's higher levels can be inaccurate at women's low levels. Ask whether your lab uses a sensitive method such as mass spectrometry, especially if results don't match how you feel.</p>`,
